 2018: 12, 2019: 18, 2020: 24, 2021: 23, 2022: 29, 2023: 35."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the change in the recycling rate from 2018 to 2023.\n(b) State the year in which the rate fell, and by how many percentage points.\n(c) Calculate the mean yearly increase from 2018 to 2023, and explain why it may not predict 2024 accurately."}]}]},"e":"12 → 35 is +23 points. The 2020→2021 change was a 1-point fall. The mean yearly increase over five intervals is 4.6 points, but extrapolating is unsound: growth is uneven and may saturate.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"line graphs","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":44,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An average rate of change smooths over the actual path; before extrapolating, check for reversals and ceilings.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the increase from 2018 to 2023, in percentage points.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Subtract the 2018 value from the 2023 value.","35 − 12."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":23,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the size of the fall in the year it decreased, in percentage points.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Compare consecutive years.","2020 was 24 and 2021 was 23."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the mean yearly increase from 2018 to 2023.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["There are five yearly intervals between 2018 and 2023.","Divide 23 by 5."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":4.6,"tolerance":0.05},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why this mean increase may not predict 2024 accurately.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Is the trend perfectly steady?","What happens to a percentage rate as it approaches its maximum?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the trend is not perfectly steady because one year fell, and growth is likely to slow as the rate approaches a practical maximum, so a simple average overstates future rises"],"keywords":[["trend"],["perfectly"],["steady"],["year"],["fell"],["growth"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the six points in the workspace and join them, then read the trend before answering (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction."},{"n":45,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A researcher collects two data sets about the same 50 students.\n\nSet P: the score on a 100-mark test.\nSet Q: the time taken to complete the test, in minutes.\n\nThe researcher writes: ''Both data sets are quantitative, so both should be shown in a histogram with the same class widths.''\n\n(a) Explain what is correct in the statement.\n(b) Explain what is incorrect in the statement.\n(c) State the class width measured in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A researcher collects two data sets about the same 50 students."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set P: the score on a 100-mark test.\nSet Q: the time taken to complete the test, in minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The researcher writes: ''Both data sets are quantitative, so both should be shown in a histogram with the same class widths.''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain what is correct in the statement.\n(b) Explain what is incorrect in the statement.\n(c) State the class width measured in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why."}]}]},"e":"Both variables are quantitative, so histograms are appropriate. But class widths must be chosen per variable: 10 marks suits a 0–100 range, while 10 minutes is too coarse for a typically 20–70 minute range, where 5-minute classes reveal the shape.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"classification of data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"Choose class widths from the range and the unit of the variable, not from a template.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Explain what is correct in the researcher''s statement.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Are both variables numerical?","Can numerical data be grouped into classes?"],"strand":"B(i)","accepted":["both variables are quantitative, so a histogram is a suitable representation for each"],"keywords":[["variables"],["quantitative"],["histogram"],["suitable"],["representation"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain what is incorrect in the statement.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Do marks and minutes have the same range?","Should the class width depend on the variable?"],"strand":"B(ii)","accepted":["the class widths must suit each variable''s range and units; using the same widths for both misrepresents the time data"],"keywords":[["class"],["widths"],["suit"],["variable''s"],["range"],["units"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State a class width in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["A width of 10 is reasonable for marks out of 100."